          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "916fa6b6-9761-3f82-a72c-1b8317cf2137",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the code uses a memory copy operation where the source and destination buffers may overlap, which can result in undefined behavior according to the C standard. The analysis detected that the source and destination may be related through data flow, increasing the risk of overlap. Additionally, there are no explicit checks or guards in the code to prevent this scenario, and the source pointer could potentially be null, which further increases the risk of undefined behavior. The destination buffer type is generic (void), and there is no information about its capacity, making it difficult to verify the safety of the operation. All these factors indicate a real risk of undefined behavior due to overlapping memory regions.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior when the source and destination memory regions may overlap, replace the memory copy operation with a function that safely handles overlapping regions, such as `memmove`. This ensures that the copy is performed correctly even if the regions overlap.\n\n```cpp\nmemmove(static_cast<void*>(buf), static_cast<void*>(file_data_ptr), size);\n```\n\nAlternatively, ensure through code logic or explicit checks that the source and destination buffers do not overlap before performing the copy. If overlap is not possible by design, document this assumption clearly in the code to aid future maintainers.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "ac75ce4a-e133-3698-a4e1-7df2a00e6596",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability \"Avoid Using Non-Cryptographic or Weak Random Number Generators\" refers to the use of random number generators that are not cryptographically secure in C++ programming language. This can lead to predictable randomness which can be exploited by an attacker to predict the outcome of the random number generator, leading to a potential security risk.\n\nIn the provided code, `std::random_shuffle` is used which is not a cryptographically secure random number generator. It uses a pseudo-random number generator to shuffle the elements, which can lead to predictable randomness.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use a cryptographically secure random number generator. In C++, this can be achieved by using the `std::shuffle` function with a random device as the random number generator.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the code:\n\n```cpp\nstd::random_device rd;\nstd::mt19937 g(rd());\n\nstd::shuffle(_file_names.begin() + _shard_start_idx_vector[_shard_id],\n             _file_names.begin() + _shard_start_idx_vector[_shard_id] + actual_shard_size_without_padding(), g);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<algorithm>` for `std::shuffle`\n- `<random>` for `std::random_device` and `std::mt19937`\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-338: Use of Cryptographically Weak PRNG](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/338.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS148264"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "62320fec-b32d-313b-998b-a7eb420b8a43",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions in C++ code. These functions are often prone to buffer overflow attacks, format string vulnerabilities, or other types of security issues. In the provided code, the `fopen` function is used, which can lead to vulnerabilities if the file name is manipulated by an attacker.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions. In the case of `fopen`, it is recommended to use file I/O functions provided by the C++ Standard Library, which are type-safe and exception-safe.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `fopen` function with the `ifstream` class from the C++ Standard Library. Here is the fixed code:\n\n```cpp\n#include <fstream>\n\nstd::ifstream file(next_file_name.c_str(), std::ios::binary);\n_current_fPtr = file.rdbuf();\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<fstream>`: for file I/O operations.\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n\n## CWE\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "11e17598-f89f-3f63-b989-18d3a0dee15f",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "The code copies data from a string's internal buffer to a destination buffer using a memory copy operation. The destination buffer's size is not known at this location, and there is no evidence that the amount of data being copied is limited to the actual capacity of the destination. This means there is a real risk of a buffer overflow if the source data is larger than the destination buffer. No guard or check is present to ensure the destination buffer is large enough. The source is a string, which is safe from overlap issues, but the main concern is the lack of bounds checking for the destination buffer. This is a common cause of security vulnerabilities such as buffer overflows.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nBefore copying data, ensure that the destination buffer is large enough to hold the source data. If the size of the destination buffer is known, compare it to the size of the source data and only copy up to the minimum of the two. For example:\n\n```cpp\nsize_t dest_capacity = /* actual size of buff */;\nsize_t src_size = _single_feature.bytes_list().value()[0].size();\nsize_t copy_size = (src_size < dest_capacity) ? src_size : dest_capacity;\nmemcpy(buff, _single_feature.bytes_list().value()[0].c_str(), copy_size);\n```\n\nIf the destination buffer's size is not known, refactor the code to pass the buffer size as an argument or use a safer container (such as std::vector or std::array) that tracks its own size. Never copy more data than the destination can hold.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "c7c51099-c91e-31c1-b2ca-a4094aa50e58",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nIn C++ programming, the vulnerability \"Avoid Using Non-Cryptographic or Weak Random Number Generators\" refers to the use of non-cryptographic or weak random number generators to generate values that are intended to be unpredictable. These values could be used in security-sensitive context, such as generating random passwords, cryptographic keys, or other similar use-cases. If a weak or non-cryptographic random number generator is used, it could lead to predictability in the generated values, which could be exploited by an attacker.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use a strong, cryptographic random number generator. In C++, this could be achieved by using the `std::random_device` class from the `<random>` library, which is designed to produce non-deterministic random numbers.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple example of how to use `std::random_device` to generate a random number:\n\n```cpp\n#include <random>\n\nstd::random_device rd;  // Will be used to obtain a seed for the random number engine\nstd::mt19937 gen(rd()); // Standard mersenne_twister_engine seeded with rd()\nstd::uniform_int_distribution<> distrib(1, 6);\n\nint random_number = distrib(gen);\n```\n\nIn this example, `std::random_device` is used to seed the Mersenne Twister pseudo-random number generator (`std::mt19937`). Then, a uniform distribution is created, which is used to generate the random number.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe above code example requires the `<random>` library.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-338: Use of Cryptographically Weak PRNG](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/338.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
