            {
              "id": "386c9c85-c1bf-32ca-a55c-9812987d848c",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nIn C++ programming, the vulnerability \"Avoid Using Non-Cryptographic or Weak Random Number Generators\" refers to the use of random number generators that are not cryptographically secure. This can lead to predictable randomness which can be exploited by an attacker to predict the outcome of the random number generation. In the provided code, `std::random_shuffle` is used which is not a cryptographically secure random number generator.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use a cryptographically secure random number generator. In C++, this can be achieved by using `std::shuffle` with a random device as the random number generator.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the provided code:\n\n```cpp\n#include <random>\n// ...\nstd::random_device rd;\nstd::mt19937 g(rd());\n\nstd::shuffle(_file_names.begin() + _shard_start_idx_vector[_shard_id],\n             _file_names.begin() + _shard_end_idx_vector[_shard_id], g);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<random>`: for `std::random_device` and `std::mt19937`\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-338: Use of Cryptographically Weak PRNG](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/338.html)"
