            {
              "id": "e471a425-90c1-36b6-8fbd-e9ddadbf5c5a",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Prohibited C Function Identified\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions in C++ code. These functions are often older and do not perform bounds checking or other necessary safety measures, leading to potential buffer overflows, memory corruption, or other security issues. In this case, the function `std::strerror` is used, which is not thread-safe and can lead to race conditions if used in a multi-threaded context.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer, modern alternatives to these functions. In the case of `std::strerror`, a thread-safe alternative such as `std::strerror_s` or `std::strerror_r` should be used instead. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `std::strerror` function with a thread-safe alternative. Here is an example of how to do this:\n\n```cpp\n#include <string.h>\n\nchar buf[1024];\nif(strerror_r(errno, buf, sizeof(buf)) == 0) {\n    ERR(\"Seek operation failed for \" + _last_file_path + \": \" + buf);\n} else {\n    ERR(\"Seek operation failed for \" + _last_file_path + \": Unknown error\");\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following libraries:\n\n- `<string.h>` for `strerror_r` function\n- `<errno.h>` for `errno`\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [CWE-477: Use of Obsolete Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/477.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
