            {
              "id": "0c3be20c-4c43-3412-98d2-4a569d068ff4",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Prohibited C Function Identified\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions in C++ code. These functions are often older and do not perform necessary checks, such as bounds checking, which can lead to vulnerabilities such as buffer overflows. In this case, the `fopen` function is used, which can lead to vulnerabilities if the file path is not properly validated or sanitized.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions provided by the C++ Standard Library. In this case, the `std::ifstream` class can be used to open files in a safer manner. Additionally, always validate and sanitize user input to prevent path traversal and other types of attacks.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `fopen` function with the `std::ifstream` class:\n\n```cpp\n#include <fstream>\n\nstd::ifstream _current_file(file_path, std::ios::binary);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n- `<fstream>`\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
