              "id": "387cb109-81df-3b1e-bcb0-9fcf358edda9",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Prohibited C Function Found\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of certain C library functions that are considered unsafe due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other security issues. These functions are often prohibited in secure coding standards. In the provided code, the function `std::strerror` is used, which is not thread-safe and can cause data races in multi-threaded programs.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions. In the case of `std::strerror`, a safer alternative is `std::strerror_s` or `std::strerror_r` which are thread-safe.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the code:\n\n```cpp\nchar buffer[256];\nif (strerror_s(buffer, sizeof(buffer), errno) != 0) {\n    // Handle error\n}\nERR(\"Could not open file \" + file_path + \": \" + buffer);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<cstring>` for `std::strerror`\n- `<cerrno>` for `errno`\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
