            {
              "id": "79b42e87-26f5-3283-af11-da787de96fa9",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nIn C++ programming, the vulnerability \"Avoid Using Non-Cryptographic or Weak Random Number Generators\" refers to the use of random number generators that are not cryptographically secure. This can lead to predictable randomness which can be exploited by an attacker to predict the outcome of the random number generator, leading to a potential security breach.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, `std::random_shuffle` is used which is not a cryptographically secure random number generator. It uses a pseudo-random number generator and can produce predictable outcomes.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use a cryptographically secure random number generator. In C++, this can be achieved by using functions provided by the `<random>` library such as `std::random_device` or `std::mt19937` with a proper seed.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the code:\n\n```cpp\n#include <random>\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstd::mt19937 rng(std::random_device{}());\nstd::shuffle(_file_names.begin() + _shard_start_idx_vector[_shard_id],\n             _file_names.begin() + _shard_end_idx_vector[_shard_id], rng);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<random>`: for `std::mt19937` and `std::random_device`\n- `<algorithm>`: for `std::shuffle`\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-338: Use of Cryptographically Weak PRNG](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/338.html)"
