            {
              "id": "c749d6e4-0354-3e79-8878-601ca7417ac4",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as high risk because the destination buffer's capacity is not known at the point of the memory copy operation, and the number of bytes being copied is not proven to be within the bounds of the destination buffer. The source is a C++ string's internal buffer, which is safe from overlap concerns, and neither the source nor the destination is likely to be null. However, since the destination is a pointer and there is no evidence that its size is sufficient to hold the copied data, there is a real risk of buffer overflow. No guard or check is present to ensure the destination buffer is large enough for the data being copied. This makes the issue a genuine vulnerability that could lead to memory corruption or security issues.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nBefore performing the memory copy, ensure that the destination buffer is large enough to hold the data being copied. This can be done by checking the size of the destination buffer against the size of the source data. If the destination buffer is dynamically allocated, allocate it with at least the size of the source data. For example:\n\n```cpp\nsize_t data_size = image_proto.byte_data().size();\n// Ensure buff is allocated with at least data_size bytes\nif (buff == nullptr || buff_size < data_size) {\n    // Handle error: insufficient buffer size\n    // e.g., allocate/reallocate buff or return an error\n}\nmemcpy(buff, image_proto.byte_data().c_str(), data_size);\n```\n\nIf the buffer is statically allocated, ensure that its size is at least as large as the data being copied, or use a safer function that limits the copy to the buffer's capacity.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)\n\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
